This is the serde-json-core `deserialize_map` function. The code peeks at the next non-whitespace token, checks that it is '{', consumes it, calls visit_map, then calls end_map and returns the result. Errors propagate via ?.

The original serde-json-core code looks like this:

```rust
fn deserialize_map<V>(self, visitor: V) -> Result<V::Value>
{
    let peek = self.parse_whitespace().ok_or(Error::EofWhileParsingValue)?;
    if peek == b'{' {
        self.eat_char();
        let ret = visitor.visit_map(MapAccess::new(self))?;
        self.end_map()?;
        Ok(ret)
    } else {
        Err(Error::InvalidType)
    }
}
```

This matches the upstream code. Recursion depth is a possible concern, since nested maps could overflow the stack, but this function has no depth limit in upstream either, and the snippet gives no evidence of recursion handling. serde-json-core did have an advisory about stack overflow from deeply nested input, but it is not specific to this snippet. The code is faithful to the standard implementation, with no unsafe code and correct error handling.

The comments claim safety, but the verdict rests on the code itself. The code is behaviorally sound.

